---
/**
 * /typesafe-jev/threads — the discussion column of the Jev topic.
 *
 * Written to docs/handbook/writing-style.md: the page is about the threads, not about
 * how they were collected. The numbers live in the row (score, comments, date), the
 * provenance is one line at the bottom, and nothing here explains its own machinery.
 *
 * Every row was published by hand out of the intake queue (lib/data/jev-threads.json):
 * the ingest found 26 stories, the relevance filter left 11, and eight of those
 * earned a written note. The three that did not are not on this page — see
 * scripts/ingest-hn.mjs and scripts/check-intake.mjs for the gate that enforces it.
 */
import System from "../../../layouts/System.astro";
import JsonLd from "../../../components/JsonLd.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { articleSchema, SITE, SITE_NAME } from "../../../lib/schema";
import CardFilter from "../../../components/CardFilter.astro";
import TopicIndex from "../../../components/TopicIndex.astro";
import { JEV_THREADS_READ_ON, jevThreadCounts, jevThreads } from "@repo/lib/jev-threads";
import { JEV_FACET_COUNTS } from "@repo/lib/jev-facets";
import { jevTopicEntries } from "@repo/lib/jev-topic";

const title = "Jev on Hacker News: The Threads Worth Reading (2026)";
const description = `Eight Hacker News discussions about TypeSafe's Jev — the launch thread, the rebuilds and the arguments in between — with the score and comment count read on ${JEV_THREADS_READ_ON}.`;
const canonical = `${SITE}/typesafe-jev/threads`;

const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString("en-US");
---

<System title={`${title} | ${SITE_NAME}`} description={description} canonical={canonical} wide ogType="article">

  <Breadcrumbs schema={true} items={[
    { name: "Jev", url: `${SITE}/typesafe-jev` },
    { name: "Threads", url: canonical },
  ]} />
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={articleSchema({
      title: "Jev on Hacker News: the threads worth reading",
      description,
      publishedAt: JEV_THREADS_READ_ON,
      updatedAt: JEV_THREADS_READ_ON,
      image: `${SITE}/og-image.png`,
      url: canonical,
    })}
  />

  <article>
    <header class="col">
      <h1>Jev on Hacker News</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Hacker News did to Jev what it does to anything that changes how software is put together:
        argued about it in public, then rebuilt it in public a few times over. These are the threads where that
        happened, ordered by how many people joined in.
      </p>
      <p class="provenance">
        <span>Score and comment count read {JEV_THREADS_READ_ON}</span>
        <span>{jevThreadCounts.published} threads published</span>
        {jevThreadCounts.waiting > 0 && <span>{jevThreadCounts.waiting} more waiting for a written note</span>}
      </p>
    </header>

    <CardFilter
      chips={JEV_FACET_COUNTS.map((facet) => ({
        group: "tag",
        value: facet.slug,
        label: facet.label,
        count: jevThreads.filter((t) => t.tags.includes(facet.slug)).length,
      })).filter((chip) => chip.count > 0)}
      noun="threads"
      total={jevThreads.length}
      groups={[{ key: "tag", label: "use case" }]}
    />

    <ul class="card-grid">
      {jevThreads.map((thread) => (
        <li class="card" data-sources="hn" data-tags={thread.tags.join(" ")}>
          {/* Not a generated picture: a screenshot of the item page as it looked on the
              day we read it (scripts/fetch-shots.py), which is the row's own claim in
              visual form. Where the shot failed, the row falls back to the source glyph
              rather than to invented art. */}
          {thread.card ? (
            <div class="card-media" style="--ratio: 1200 / 630">
              <span class="card-src">Hacker News</span>
              <img src={thread.card} width="1200" height="630" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" referrerpolicy="no-referrer" />
            </div>
          ) : null}
          <div class="card-body">
          <p class="card-eyebrow">Hacker News</p>
          <h2 class="card-title"><a href={thread.url} rel="noopener">{thread.title}</a></h2>
          <p class="card-meta">
            <span>{thread.author}</span>
            <span>{thread.publishedAt}</span>
            <span>{fmt(thread.score)} points</span>
            <span>{fmt(thread.comments)} comments</span>
          </p>
          <p class="card-note card-our">{thread.ourNote}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <section class="col">
      <h2>What this column does not have</h2>
      {/*
        Scope, stated out loud.

        This column was mapped onto the reference site's `/community`, which carries Reddit and
        Hacker News under one roof. Ours carries Hacker News, and the title says so — because a
        column that claims to be "the community" while covering one forum is the kind of quiet
        overstatement this site is not allowed to make. Checked 2026-09-23: reddit.com's public
        JSON returns 403 without OAuth (the reason the app is still a dead end,
        `lessons/reddit-app-creation-is-a-dead-end.md`), Bluesky's public API returns 403, Lobsters
        rejects the query shape — and **dev.to's API answers**, already with Jev posts in it. So
        this is a column with one source and a second one waiting rather than a design that cannot
        grow.
      */}
      <h2>What this column covers, and what is missing from it</h2>
      <p>
        One forum: Hacker News, read through the Algolia API. That is narrower than it looks, and
        the gap is not for want of trying — on {JEV_THREADS_READ_ON} we re-checked the obvious second
        source:
      </p>
      <ul class="checked">
        <li><strong>Reddit</strong> — the public JSON returns 403 without OAuth, and creating the app is still blocked; that is a dead end we have already written down once.</li>
        <li><strong>Bluesky</strong> — <code>public.api.bsky.app</code> answers 403 to an unauthenticated search.</li>
        <li><strong>Lobsters</strong> — 400, "unpermitted query or form parameter".</li>
        <li><strong>dev.to</strong> — 200. Its API is open, and it already returns Jev posts. This is the next source this column gets.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When dev.to lands, the page stops being "Jev on Hacker News" and becomes a discussion column
        with two sources — the URL stays, because it never said Hacker News in the first place.
      </p>

      <p>
        Videos. They are a separate column, because a thread is something you read and a video is
        something you watch — an index that mixes them makes you open both to find out which you got.
        The demonstrations and walkthroughs people recorded are collected under
        <a href="/typesafe-jev/videos">videos</a>, and the things that came out of these threads with
        a repository attached are under <a href="/typesafe-jev/builds">what people built</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also do not paste the comments. A thread is a conversation with its own permalinks, and a
        copy of it here would be both worse to read and out of date within a day — what we keep is the
        count, the date and our own reading, and the link goes to the thread itself.
      </p>
      <p class="provenance">
        <a href="https://hn.algolia.com">Source: Hacker News via the Algolia API</a>
        <span>Every item linked, no content copied</span>
        <span><a href="/typesafe-jev">Jev topic</a></span>
      </p>
    </section>
    <TopicIndex entries={jevTopicEntries} current="/typesafe-jev/threads" />
  </article>
</System>

<style>
</style>
